Trade-Offs in Container Network Policies: Evaluating Linear, Per-Endpoint, and Node-Global Dataplanes in High-Churn Clusters
Target Venue: USENIX NSDI / USENIX ATC / ACM SIGCOMM Style Systems Paper
Authors: Antonio Ojea, Michael Aspinwall, et al.

Abstractß
Modern distributed systems, including parallel reinforcement learning rollouts, AI agent execution sandboxes, and serverless function graphs, rely on the dynamic creation of short-lived container instances at high concurrency (e.g., 500 QPS sustained bursts). Because these workloads frequently execute untrusted, model-generated, or multi-tenant code, the underlying container orchestration framework must enforce strict, zero-trust network policies from the instant of socket binding. Over the past decade, cloud-native container networking has relied primarily on two established architectural paradigms: first-generation linear packet filters (iptables) and second-generation ahead-of-time per-endpoint compilers (e.g., eBPF / Cilium). In this paper, we show that existing architectures navigate a fundamental Container Network Policy Trade-Off Space, presenting intrinsic compromises between (1) Packet-Path Forwarding Efficiency, (2) Container Provisioning Agility, and (3) Control-Plane API Server Scalability. While ahead-of-time per-endpoint eBPF compilation eliminates packet-time classification overhead and excels when communication graphs are hierarchical or known in advance (1.31s P50 startup), we reveal that it trades off provisioning agility under dynamic peer-to-peer (Full Mesh) topologies, incurring an O(N × P) per-endpoint map state explosion, identity allocator exhaustion, and CNI concurrency serialization.
To navigate these trade-offs for high-churn, ephemeral AI sandboxes and serverless clusters, we propose kube-network-policies (KNP), a novel third-generation architecture based on Synchronous Node-Global Set Matching. KNP evaluates connection-setup packets against node-shared kernel hash sets (nft_set) with connection tracking (conntrack) reuse, achieving topology-invariant O(1) container provisioning (2.35s P50, 9.72s P99) across dense mesh graphs. To overcome the resulting API server watch fan-out pressure, KNP combines synchronous Node Resource Interface (NRI) runtime hooks with upstream control-plane streaming innovations (etcd RangeStream KEP-5966, Watch-List streaming KEP-3157, Stale Controller Mitigation KEP-5647, and disk-backed bbolt caching), eliminating control-plane queue starvation while delivering instantaneous zero-trust isolation.

1. Introduction
1.1 Motivation & The Ephemeral Workload Shift
Kubernetes has become the standard orchestration platform for containerized distributed systems, relying on the declarative NetworkPolicy API to enforce multi-tenant security isolation. Historically, cloud-native deployments predominantly comprised long-lived, trusted microservices whose operational lifecycles spanned days, weeks, or months. In this classical operating regime, the control-plane programming latency and dataplane filter instantiation overhead incurred during container creation were amortized over thousands of operational hours.
Modern agentic AI and serverless workflows, such as parallel reinforcement learning rollouts, tool-use sandboxes, and code interpretation engines, challenge these assumptions. These dynamic workloads exhibit three orthogonal systems requirements:
Extreme Creation Bursts: Thousands of ephemeral sandboxes are provisioned concurrently (e.g., 500 QPS sustained creation, bursting to 5,000 pods in seconds).
Sub-Minute Ephemerality: Workload execution lifecycles range from several seconds to minutes, making multi-second dataplane programming delays a dominant fraction of end-to-end task turnaround time.
Strict Zero-Trust Isolation: Because execution sandboxes execute untrusted user code or unverified model-generated artifacts, containers must be sealed within microsegmented security perimeters from the millisecond of network interface instantiation.
1.2 The Evolution of Container Network Policy Architectures
To satisfy network policy isolation, cloud-native Container Network Interface (CNI) dataplanes have progressed through three distinct architectural generations:
Generation 1 (Legacy Baseline): Linear Packet Classification (iptables / Netfilter): Early Kubernetes networking relied on sequential Netfilter rule traversal. While universally compatible, sequential rule checking incurs an O(R) packet latency overhead with respect to total rule count R, while pod lifecycle churn triggers monolithic table replacement lock contention (iptables-restore), severely degrading control-plane responsiveness.
Generation 2 (Current Industry Standard): Ahead-of-Time (AoT) Per-Endpoint Compilation (eBPF / Cilium): To eliminate packet-time classification overhead, modern eBPF-based dataplanes project arbitrary label sets into compact integer SecurityIdentities and compile policy graphs into dedicated per-endpoint BPF bytecode and tail-call maps (cilium_policy_*). By embedding policy lookups directly at the veth/socket boundary, AoT compilation achieves sub-microsecond packet forwarding. However, this design trades off container creation latency: every newly provisioned container must allocate dedicated kernel maps, perform bytecode verification, and synchronize cluster-wide identity state.
Generation 3 (Our Proposed Architecture): Synchronous Node-Global Set Matching (kube-network-policies / KNP): To decouple container provisioning from policy graph complexity without reverting to linear filtering, we propose KNP. KNP evaluates policies at the host level against centralized kernel hash sets (nft_set in nftables) combined with connection tracking (conntrack) fast-path reuse. Rather than provisioning isolated filter graphs per interface, KNP evaluates initial connection packets (TCP SYN) against shared sets in O(1) time and commits established flows to conntrack, eliminating per-endpoint map compilation.
1.3 The Container Network Policy Trade-Off Space
No single dataplane architecture dominates across all operational dimensions. Container networking engines balance three competing design axes:
Packet-Path Forwarding Efficiency: Maximizing packet throughput and minimizing per-packet CPU traversal overhead on active streams.
Container Provisioning Agility: Minimizing the latency required to instantiate network interfaces and enforce security perimeters on newly scheduled containers (schedule-to-run latency).
Control-Plane Scalability & Watch Fan-Out: Minimizing API server serialization overhead, watch stream fan-out, and memory footprint across worker node daemons.
Under static or hierarchical topologies (e.g., Ingress → Microservice Hub-and-Spoke), Ahead-of-Time Per-Endpoint Compilation delivers an optimal balance: worker nodes enforce simple egress rules without expanding peer identities, achieving sub-second pod provisioning (1.31s P50) alongside zero packet-time classification. However, under flat or unpredictable peer-to-peer topologies (Full Mesh), per-endpoint compilation suffers an O(N × P) map state explosion, identity allocator exhaustion, and local CNI ADD concurrency bottlenecks.
Conversely, Node-Global Set Matching guarantees topology-invariant O(1) container provisioning (2.35s P50, 9.72s P99) under dense mesh graphs by eliminating per-pod map allocations. However, it shifts the system bottleneck from the kernel dataplane to the control-plane watch pipeline: because matching is performed against IP sets, every worker node daemon must maintain an active watch stream on cluster-wide pod state, creating API server watch fan-out and client queue starvation unless mitigated by runtime co-design.
1.4 Summary of Contributions
In this paper, we establish an objective, empirical taxonomy of container network policy architectures under massive burst workloads. Specifically, we make the following contributions:
Large-Scale Empirical Characterization: We conduct a comprehensive benchmark across a 720-node cluster (2,880 vCPUs) sustaining 500 QPS bursts up to 35,000 pods, evaluating the operational trade-offs and failure modes of linear Netfilter, per-endpoint eBPF compilation, and node-global set matching.
Theoretical Complexity & State Cost Models: We develop formal models quantifying per-endpoint state, cluster memory consumption, and control-plane update frequency across Asymmetric Hub-and-Spoke versus Dense Peer Mesh communication graphs.
Design & Implementation of KNP: We design, implement, and propose kube-network-policies (KNP), a novel container network policy architecture that leverages host-shared kernel hash sets, conntrack fast-path reuse, containerd NRI lifecycle hooks, and control-plane streaming (etcd RangeStream KEP-5966, Watch-List KEP-3157, Stale Controller Mitigation KEP-5647, and bbolt caching) to eliminate container provisioning delays while scaling cluster-wide state dissemination.
Actionable Systems Decision Framework: We provide an architectural decision matrix mapping workload characteristics, including lifetime ephemerality, communication topology predictability, and identity cardinality to the optimal dataplane paradigm.

2. Theoretical Foundations & Architectural Paradigms
2.1 The Kubernetes Network Policy Model & The Translation Problem
2.1.1 Declarative Semantics & Label-Matching Abstractions
The Kubernetes NetworkPolicy API provides a declarative specification for multi-tenant network isolation based on label-matching predicates. A policy object isolates a set of target workloads selected by spec.podSelector and establishes additive whitelists for incoming (ingress) and outgoing (egress) traffic:
Target Selection: spec.podSelector identifies which local pods within a namespace are governed by the policy.
Peer Matching Dimensions: Traffic permissions are defined along four orthogonal selector types:
podSelector: Matches peer pods within the same namespace based on label key-value pairs.
namespaceSelector: Matches entire namespaces based on namespace-level metadata labels.
Combined namespaceSelector AND podSelector: Matches specific pods located within specifically labeled namespaces (cross-namespace microsegmentation).
ipBlock: Specifies static CIDR blocks and CIDR exception ranges (except) for external or cluster-external egress/ingress.
ports: Restricts matching flows to specific L4 protocols (TCP, UDP, SCTP) and numeric or named port targets.
By default, container communication within a Kubernetes cluster is fully open (non-isolated). Once an endpoint is selected by at least one NetworkPolicy, it enters an isolated default-deny posture: any connection that does not explicitly match an allowed whitelist rule is dropped.
2.1.2 The Semantic Gap: Translating Labels to L3/L4 Packet Primitives
A fundamental architectural challenge in Kubernetes networking is the semantic gap between control-plane declarations and dataplane realities:
Control Plane: Expresses security policies in terms of high-level, multi-dimensional, mutable key-value metadata attached to abstract objects (Pods, Namespaces).
Kernel Dataplane: Operates strictly on physical L3/L4 packet header tuples: Source IP, Destination IP, Protocol, Source Port, and Destination Port. The Linux kernel network stack possesses no native awareness of Kubernetes pods, namespaces, or labels.
Consequently, the core responsibility of any Container Network Interface (CNI) policy engine is continuous, real-time translation: dynamically mapping abstract label-set intersections into concrete packet-filtering data structures (such as IP hashes, BPF maps, or firewall chains) as containers spawn, migrate, and terminate across the cluster.
2.1.3 API Server Watch Mechanics & Cluster-Wide State Dissemination
To perform this translation, every worker node's CNI agent requires complete, real-time visibility into the global cluster state. When a packet arrives at worker node W from source IP X addressed to destination IP Y, the local node cannot determine whether the flow is permitted solely from local state. The node must resolve:
Which pod owns IP X, and what labels does Pod X currently hold?
What namespace does Pod X belong to, and what labels does that Namespace currently possess?
Does the tuple (PodLabels(X), NamespaceLabels(X)) satisfy the ingress selectors governing Pod Y?
Because any pod in the cluster can potentially initiate a connection to a local pod, every worker node CNI agent must maintain cluster-wide watches. In standard Kubernetes client architecture (client-go), each node agent runs an Informer/Reflector pipeline that establishes long-lived HTTP/2 chunked watch streams against the kube-apiserver for three core resource types:
Pods (Cluster-wide)
Namespaces (Cluster-wide)
NetworkPolicies (Cluster-wide)
Under static enterprise workloads, this watch mechanism operates efficiently because pod creations are rare. However, under high-concurrency ephemeral AI agent workloads (e.g., 500 QPS sustained creation across a 720-node cluster), the watch dissemination pipeline becomes a massive distributed multiplier:
Watch Fan-Out Volume: A creation burst of 500 pods/sec across M = 720 worker nodes forces the kube-apiserver to serialize and broadcast 500 × 720 = 360,000 watch events per second.
Memory Footprint per Node: Every worker node daemon must maintain a complete in-memory cache of all active cluster pods and their metadata, consuming significant RAM on resource-constrained worker nodes.
Client Queue Saturation: Under burst load, client-go DeltaFIFO queues and serialization buffers back up, introducing multi-second state propagation lags between kube-apiserver and worker node daemons.
Namespace Relabeling Cascades: Mutating a single label on a namespace object invalidates the selector matching state for all pods residing within that namespace, triggering a cluster-wide re-evaluation of every policy referencing namespaceSelector.
2.1.4 The Asynchronous Race Condition: Pod Execution vs. Watch Latency
This state dissemination pipeline introduces an inherent distributed race condition: When Pod A is scheduled on Node 1, its container runtime starts executing code and sends a network packet to Pod B on Node 2. However, Node 2's CNI informer stream may not yet have received the API server watch event announcing Pod A's IP and labels. If Node 2 evaluates incoming packets before its local cache has updated, it observes an "unknown" source IP, resulting in false-positive packet drops or transient connection timeouts.
To solve this translation problem and mitigate distributed state synchronization lags, dataplanes navigate three distinct architectural paradigms:
2.2 Paradigm 1: Linear Rule Traversal (Chain-Based Model)
The earliest container firewalling architectures rely on sequential packet filter evaluation (e.g., standard Linux Netfilter / iptables chains).
Evaluation Mechanism: The CNI controller expands Kubernetes policy rules into linear chains of packet predicates. Each connection packet traversing the host network stack sequentially tests its headers against an ordered sequence of rules until a matching accept or drop verdict is reached.
Packet-Path Overhead: As the cluster scales in pod count and policy complexity, total rule count R grows linearly or quadratically. Sequential rule evaluation incurs an O(R) per-packet CPU latency penalty on active packet streams.
Mutation & Lock Contention: Modifying rules under endpoint churn requires dumping the entire kernel table to userspace, editing the rule list, and restoring the full table into the kernel. This operation acquires a global kernel mutex lock (such as xtables_lock), causing CPU saturation and serialization delays of tens of seconds under rapid pod churn.
2.3 Paradigm 2: Ahead-of-Time Per-Endpoint Compilation (Endpoint-Isolated Model)
To eliminate packet-time linear classification and global table lock contention, second-generation dataplanes adopt an ahead-of-time (AoT) compilation strategy that compiles policies directly to individual container network interfaces.
Security Identity Abstraction: Rather than evaluating raw string labels or IP addresses sequentially in the packet path, the control plane projects multi-dimensional label tuples into scalar integer Security Identities. In traditional microservices where thousands of pod replicas share templated labels (N ≪ P), identity mapping provides significant policy compression.
Endpoint-Isolated State Tables: On each worker node, the dataplane instantiates dedicated, per-endpoint lookup tables attached directly to each container interface (e.g., virtual ethernet pairs). Packet forwarding checks are performed in O(1) time by querying:
A node-wide mapping resolving remote IP addresses to their corresponding Security Identity.
A per-endpoint table mapping (Remote Security Identity, Destination Port, Protocol) tuples to forwarding verdicts.
The Provisioning Trade-Off: While AoT per-endpoint compilation achieves near-zero packet-path overhead, it tightly couples container provisioning to per-interface state allocation. Every newly scheduled container must allocate and populate its own isolated policy table before its network interface can be safely brought online. Under flat, dense peer-to-peer topologies where every container must communicate with N peers, total cluster-wide state scales multiplicatively as O(N × P), resulting in table capacity exhaustion and container creation serialization.
2.4 Paradigm 3: Our Proposed Solution (Synchronous Node-Global Set Matching / KNP)
To decouple container provisioning from policy topology density without reverting to linear rule traversal, third-generation dataplanes evaluate policies at the host level against centralized kernel hash sets combined with connection tracking (conntrack) fast-path state reuse.
Host-Shared Set Architecture: Instead of instantiating isolated filter tables per container, the dataplane compiles each NetworkPolicy label selector into a centralized kernel hash set residing in the host network namespace. A single shared set representing an allowed peer group serves all local containers simultaneously.
First-Packet SYN Evaluation: When a local container initiates or receives a connection, the dataplane performs an O(1) hash probe against the node's shared set strictly on the initial synchronization packet (TCP SYN / ct state new).
Conntrack Fast-Path Reuse: Once the initial handshake is validated against the set, the flow is committed to the Linux kernel connection tracking table (conntrack). All subsequent data packets in the established TCP stream bypass set evaluation entirely (ct state established,related accept), achieving line-rate throughput without per-packet hashing overhead.
Synchronous Runtime Lifecycle Interception: To eliminate race conditions where a newly spawned container executes without active policy enforcement, node-global engines intercept container sandbox creation synchronously via runtime lifecycle hooks (e.g., Node Resource Interface / NRI) before unpausing the container process.
The Control-Plane State Dissemination Trade-Off: The primary trade-off of node-global set matching lies in control-plane state propagation: because policies match against dynamic IP sets rather than abstract integer identities, every worker node daemon must maintain an active watch stream on cluster-wide pod lifecycle events. In large clusters with hundreds of worker nodes, simultaneous watch streams processing high-frequency churn can saturate API server client queues unless mitigated by runtime co-design.

3. Theoretical Foundations: The Cost of Information Dissemination in Kubernetes
To understand why container network policy architectures scale or fail under high churn, we must first analyze the fundamental physics of information dissemination in a distributed system. Kubernetes network policy enforcement is not merely a local packet classification problem; it is a distributed state consistency problem. Maintaining eventual consistency of dynamic security perimeters across hundreds of independent worker nodes incurs substantial control-plane, network, memory, and kernel serialization overheads.
3.1 Information Dissemination: The Watch and Dataplane Pipelines
Enforcing network policies in Kubernetes requires maintaining continuous synchronization between the centralized control plane and distributed kernel dataplanes across worker nodes. This state dissemination path divides cleanly into two primary operational pipelines:
+─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────+
|                         CONTROL-PLANE WATCH PIPELINE                        |
|                                                                             |
|      [Pod Mutation / IPAM] ──► [etcd] ──► [kube-apiserver WatchCache]       |
|                                                     │                       |
|                             Broadcast Fan-Out (M)   │                       |
|                                                     ▼                       |
|                                           [M HTTP/2 Watch Streams]          |
+─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────+
                                                      │ (M * Q events/sec)
                                                      ▼
+─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────+
|                         LOCAL NODE DATAPLANE PIPELINE                       |
|                                                                             |
|      [Node CNI Agent / Informer Cache] ──► [Policy Selector Evaluation]     |
|                                                     │                       |
|                             Kernel Translation      │                       |
|                                                     ▼                       |
|                     [Linux Kernel Dataplane: nft_set / BPF Maps]            |
|                                                     │                       |
|                                                     ▼                       |
|                             [Active Packet Path: SYN / conntrack]           |
+─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────+
The Control-Plane Watch Pipeline: When a container is provisioned and assigned an IP address, the change is committed to etcd and indexed by the kube-apiserver. The API server then serializes and broadcasts the event across M long-lived HTTP/2 watch streams to worker node daemons, creating a broadcast multiplier of M * Q events/second under cluster-wide pod churn.
The Local Dataplane Pipeline: Each worker node daemon receives the watch event, evaluates active policy selectors (matching podSelector and namespaceSelector label sets against cached state), and translates permitted endpoints into concrete kernel packet-filtering structures by inserting IP addresses into host-shared nft_set kernel hash sets (KNP) or updating per-endpoint BPF maps (Cilium).
3.2 Mathematical Formulation of Dissemination Costs & Scaling Bounds
Maintaining this distributed pipeline introduces four fundamental scaling bounds that govern cluster scalability:
3.2.1 Control-Plane Broadcast Fan-Out & Serialization Cost
Let Q denote the sustained pod creation rate (QPS), M denote the number of worker nodes, and S_event denote the average serialized size of a Pod watch event (~2.5 KB in JSON format). The aggregate outbound watch bandwidth B_egress emitted by kube-apiserver is:
B_egress = Q * M * S_event
Under our benchmark scale of Q = 500 pods/sec across M = 720 worker nodes:
Total watch events delivered: 500 * 720 = 360,000 events/second.
Outbound serialization bandwidth: 500 * 720 * 2.5 KB = 900 MB/second (~7.2 Gbps).
This broadcast multiplier forces the master node to spend massive CPU time on JSON serialization and context switching, saturating API server outbound buffers and triggering HTTP/2 flow-control window throttling.
3.2.2 Worker Node Cache Redundancy & Memory Footprint
Let P denote the total active sandbox pods in the cluster, and S_pod_mem denote the resident memory allocated by a Go runtime to store a deserialized v1.Pod struct with labels and index metadata (~15 KB per pod in client-go cache).
If every worker node daemon watches the entire cluster, the memory allocated per node daemon is:
Memory_node = P * S_pod_mem
For P = 35,000 pods:
Memory per worker node daemon: 35,000 * 15 KB ≈ 525 MB RAM.
Cluster-wide memory redundancy: M * P * S_pod_mem = 720 * 525 MB ≈ 378 GB RAM.
On edge computing nodes or low-spec virtual machines (e.g., 4 GB to 16 GB RAM), dedicating over 500 MB strictly to network policy pod caches creates significant memory pressure.
3.2.3 Distributed Propagation Latency & Queuing Delay
The total end-to-end state dissemination latency tau_prop is the sum of pipeline stages:
tau_prop = tau_etcd + tau_apiserver + tau_net + tau_fifo + tau_reconcile + tau_kernel
Under light load, tau_prop is dominated by network transmission (10-50 ms). However, under 500 QPS burst load, client-go DeltaFIFO queues back up across all 720 daemons. When tau_fifo swells to tens of seconds, remote nodes evaluate incoming packets against stale security tables, creating the asynchronous race condition where valid packets are dropped as unauthorized.
3.2.4 Cascading Invalidation Multipliers (Namespace & Multi-Selector Relabeling)
When security rules match on namespaceSelector, modifying a single label on a Namespace object containing p_ns pods invalidates the selector matching state for all p_ns pods simultaneously. For K policies active in the cluster, a single namespace mutation triggers:
Re-evaluations = p_ns * K * M
operations across the cluster. In large multi-tenant clusters, a single namespace update can trigger millions of combinatorial selector checks, causing CPU spikes across all worker daemons.
3.3 Architectural Trade-Offs: The Status Quo (AoT Compilation) vs. Our Proposed Solution (KNP)
To manage this distributed complexity, existing industry-standard engines and our proposed KNP architecture make fundamentally opposing design choices:
[Status Quo / Existing Standard: Local Watch + Endpoint-Isolated Maps (e.g., Cilium)]
- Pod Watch Scope: NODE-LOCAL for Pods, but CLUSTER-WIDE for Identities (CiliumIdentity CRDs)
- API Server Watch Deliveries:
    * Topology A & B (Hierarchical): O(P) [Node-scoped; remote identities unexpanded on workers]
    * Topology C (Full Mesh, N = P): O(M * N) = O(M * P) [All M nodes must watch all N CRDs]
- Dataplane State Model: Dedicated BPF Maps per Container Interface
- Pod Startup Dataplane Cost: O(N) entries compiled into each local container map
- Scaling Bottlenecks: Quadratic O(N * P) map explosion, CRD watch fan-out, & CNI lock contention in peer meshes

[Our Proposed Architecture: Global Watch + Node-Shared Kernel Sets (KNP)]
- Pod Watch Scope: CLUSTER-WIDE for Pods (Optimized via KEP-5966 & KEP-3157 Streaming)
- API Server Watch Deliveries: O(M * P) across all topologies (Core Pod objects)
- Dataplane State Model: 1 Shared Kernel Set (nft_set) per node of size N
- Pod Startup Dataplane Cost: O(1) (No per-endpoint map allocation; NRI runtime hooks)
- Scaling Advantage: Topology-invariant container creation; complete mesh resilience
The Status Quo (Ahead-of-Time Endpoint Compilation): Under hierarchical topologies (Topology A and B), Cilium restricts pod watches to the local node, keeping watch overhead low (O(P)). However, under flat peer-to-peer topologies (Topology C) with dynamic per-pod identities (N ≈ P), every worker node's cilium-agent must watch all N cluster-wide CiliumIdentity CRDs to populate its local cilium_ipcache map. Consequently, Cilium's watch fan-out also explodes to O(M * N) = O(M * P) (25.2 million deliveries at 35,000 pods across 720 nodes), compounded by the heavy serialization and etcd write transaction overhead of Custom Resources. Furthermore, each container must allocate, verify, and populate dedicated BPF tables during interface instantiation, triggering severe map capacity exhaustion (2N > 65,536).
Our Proposed Architecture (KNP / Node-Global Sets): Directly evaluates core Pod object metadata against host-shared kernel hash sets (nft_set) with conntrack fast-path reuse, achieving guaranteed O(1) container provisioning agility in the kernel dataplane. To prevent the resulting M * P core pod watch fan-out from bottlenecking the control plane, KNP co-designs with upstream streaming advancements (etcd RangeStream KEP-5966, Watch-List streaming KEP-3157, and local bbolt disk caching).
3.4 Formal Dataplane Cost Models: Compilation vs. Set Evaluation
To quantify how these strategies diverge across real-world workloads, we formulate theoretical cost models for three canonical traffic topologies.
3.4.1 Formal Parameters & System Notation
Consider a Kubernetes cluster characterized by:
M: Number of worker nodes hosting container workloads (M = 720).
P: Total number of active sandbox pods provisioned in the cluster (P = 35,000).
k: Average sandbox pod density per worker node, defined as k = P / M (k ≈ 48.6 pods per node).
N: Cardinality of distinct security identities or label tuples present across sandbox workloads (1 ≤ N ≤ P).
G: Number of dedicated ingress or gateway proxy instances (G = 2).
S_aot: Total policy entries instantiated cluster-wide across all endpoint interfaces.
S_global: Total kernel set elements maintained cluster-wide across all worker nodes.
U_node: Number of kernel map or set modification operations executed on a single worker node when a new pod is provisioned.
3.4.2 Topology A: Open Gateway (Baseline Model)
In the baseline open gateway topology, all sandbox pods communicate exclusively with an external egress gateway (or cluster DNS), while the gateway accepts traffic from arbitrary IP addresses (0.0.0.0/0):
Policy Definition: Sandboxes permit egress only to the gateway IP and CoreDNS (UDP/TCP:53); the gateway permits unrestricted ingress.
Ahead-of-Time Endpoint Complexity: Each sandbox endpoint requires a fixed baseline of c_base = 3 policy entries (Gateway IP, DNS TCP:53, DNS UDP:53). Because no pod selector expands the dynamic sandbox peer group, zero endpoints expand N.
Total Cluster State: S_aot^(A) = P * c_base = 3P ≈ 105,000 entries.
State per Worker Node: k * c_base ≈ 146 entries.
Churn Overhead: Provisioning a new sandbox requires inserting 3 entries into its local table (U_node = 3).
Node-Global Set Complexity: Each node maintains a static hash set containing the gateway and DNS addresses (size 3).
Total Cluster State: S_global^(A) = M * 3 = 2,160 elements.
Scaling Property: Both architectures exhibit O(1) state complexity per endpoint with respect to cluster identity cardinality N.
3.4.3 Topology B: Asymmetric Microsegmentation (Hub & Spoke)
In structured zero-trust architectures, sandboxes are strictly isolated behind an ingress blast wall: sandboxes may only egress to the gateway, but the gateway enforces a strict ingress selector matching role: sandbox:
Policy Definition: Sandboxes egress to gateway; gateway accepts ingress only from verified sandbox endpoints.
Ahead-of-Time Endpoint Complexity:
The P sandbox endpoints still require only c_base = 3 egress entries to the gateway.
The G gateway endpoints must evaluate all N distinct sandbox identities on ingress, requiring G * N entries.
Total Cluster State: S_aot^(B) = (G * N) + 3P.
For N = 35,000 and G = 2: S_aot^(B) = 70,000 + 105,000 = 175,000 entries.
The Asymmetry Blast Wall: In this topology, the M - G_nodes = 719 worker nodes hosting the sandbox workloads never expand N. The combinatorial identity expansion is isolated entirely to the dedicated nodes hosting the gateway pods. Consequently, worker node container provisioning latency remains completely flat across N = 7 to N = 35,000 (1.31s to 1.53s P50).
Node-Global Set Complexity: Worker nodes maintain a static set of size 3; the gateway nodes maintain a set of size N.
Total Cluster State: S_global^(B) = (M - 1) * 3 + N ≈ 2,157 + N elements.
3.4.4 Topology C: Flat Bidirectional Mesh (Full Peer-to-Peer)
In collaborative agentic workflows, multi-agent reasoning graphs, or peer-to-peer distributed execution, any sandbox pod may initiate or receive connections from any other sandbox pod (ingress.from: role: sandbox, egress.to: role: sandbox):
Policy Definition: Full bidirectional mesh where all P sandboxes are permitted to communicate with the entire peer group.
Ahead-of-Time Endpoint Complexity:
Because each endpoint enforces isolated filtering, every single sandbox container must expand all N peer identities for both ingress and egress rules (2N entries per endpoint).
State per Endpoint: E_aot = 2N entries.
State per Worker Node: k * 2N = (P / M) * 2N ≈ 97.2 * N entries.
Total Cluster State: S_aot^(C) = P * 2N = 2 * N * P.
The Quadratic State Explosion:
At N = 3,500: S_aot^(C) = 35,000 * 7,000 = 245,000,000 policy entries.
At N = 35,000: S_aot^(C) = 35,000 * 70,000 = 2,450,000,000 policy entries (2.45 billion entries).
Identity Watch Fan-Out Collapse: In a full mesh with dynamic per-pod identities (N = P), Cilium can no longer restrict its watch scope to local pods. Because every node daemon must resolve the security identity of every remote pod in the cluster, all M nodes must watch all N cluster-wide CiliumIdentity Custom Resources. The resulting API server watch fan-out reaches M * N = M * P = 25.2 million watch deliveries, imposing severe custom resource serialization and etcd transaction overhead on top of local BPF map capacity overflow.
Node-Global Set Complexity:
Each worker node maintains exactly one shared hash set of size N in its root network namespace.
Total Cluster State: S_global^(C) = M * N = 720 * N elements (25.2 million entries at N = 35,000, a 97-fold memory reduction compared to endpoint-isolated tables).
Lookups in Packet Path: O(1) hash table probe strictly on initial SYN packet.
Churn Decoupling: A new pod creation requires exactly U_node = 1 atomic kernel set insertion per node, completely decoupling update cost from local pod density k.
