4. Testbed & Experimental Methodology
4.1 Cluster Architecture & Hardware Scale
To evaluate container network policy scaling under extreme burst conditions, we provisioned an enterprise-grade testbed matching production AI agent execution environments:
720 Worker Nodes: Google Cloud n2-standard-4 virtual machines (4 vCPUs, 16 GB RAM, Ubuntu 22.04 LTS kernel 5.15), providing 2,880 total worker vCPUs.
1 Dedicated Control Plane Node: c4-standard-96 compute-optimized instance (96 vCPUs, 384 GB RAM, Google Cloud Hyperdisk Balanced provisioned at 10,000 IOPS and 1,000 MB/s throughput) to ensure etcd and kube-apiserver disk I/O are never an artificial bottleneck.
Dedicated Telemetry Node: c4-standard-96 hosting a high-frequency Prometheus server scraping 720 node-exporters, CNI daemons, and kube-apiserver metrics at 1s intervals.
Runtime Environment: Containerd 2.2.4 + runc 1.3.5 with the Node Resource Interface (NRI) enabled across all nodes. Kubelet configuration tuned to kubeAPIQPS: 50 and registryPullQPS: 50.
Underlay Networking: kOps private VPC topology with VXLAN encapsulation and 100 Gbps inter-node networking bandwidth.
4.2 Concrete Systems Under Test & Architectural Limitation Hypotheses
We evaluate representative production implementations of the two primary modern paradigms:
Cilium (Representative Ahead-of-Time eBPF Implementation):
Architecture: Implements the endpoint-isolated AoT compilation model using eBPF programs attached to tc (Traffic Control) and socket hooks, with dedicated per-endpoint BPF maps (cilium_policy_<ep_id>). Label combinations are translated into cluster-wide CiliumIdentity custom resources managed by cilium-operator.
Architectural Limitation Hypotheses:
Identity Allocator Ceiling: Cilium utilizes a 24-bit numeric space with reserved ranges for ClusterMesh and special entities, establishing a practical cluster ceiling of 65,280 identities. Under high-churn workloads with unique per-sandbox labels, allocator exhaustion is predicted to block container creation.
BPF Map Table Overflow: Each endpoint's policy map is statically sized at load time (bpf-policy-map-max, default 16,384, tuned to 65,536). Under dense full-mesh topologies (N = 35,000), required entries (2N = 70,000) are predicted to exceed map capacity, triggering packet drops.
CNI ADD Concurrency Bottleneck: Provisioning parallel containers requires local agent serialization for map allocation and bytecode verification, predicted to trigger client-side rate-limiting (HTTP 429 putEndpointIdTooManyRequests).
kube-network-policies / KNP (Our Proposed Node-Global Set Architecture):
Architecture: Implements host-shared set matching using Linux nftables hash sets (nft_set), kernel conntrack flow state reuse, and containerd Node Resource Interface (NRI) socket interception.
Architectural Limitation Hypotheses:
Watch Fan-Out & API Server Queue Starvation: Because node daemons match on dynamic IP sets, all M = 720 worker nodes simultaneously watch cluster-wide pod events. Under 500 QPS burst churn, informer watch streams are predicted to experience API server queue starvation, ballooning container startup delay unless mitigated by synchronous container runtime interception (NRI) or aggregated caching architectures.
4.3 Benchmark Harness & Latency Metric Decomposition
All experiments are orchestrated using the Kubernetes SIG-Scalability benchmark harness, ClusterLoader2, operating in high-throughput scheduler mode (high-throughput-scheduler). We evaluate burst creation rates across 50, 100, 200, and 500 QPS up to a target scale of 35,000 concurrent sandbox pods (~48.6 pods per node).
We formally decompose end-to-end pod startup latency into three non-overlapping phases:
create_to_schedule: Elapsed time from raw API object submission to kube-apiserver until the kube-scheduler assigns the pod to a target node (PodScheduled condition).
schedule_to_run: Elapsed time from node binding until the local Kubelet, CRI runtime (run_pod_sandbox), NRI lifecycle hook, CNI network setup, and container entrypoint completion (PodReady condition).
total_pod_startup: The composite end-to-end latency (T_total = T_sched + T_run).
4.4 Policy Scenario Manifests & Reproducibility Matrix
To ensure full reproducibility, all network policy manifests and benchmark configurations are maintained in the companion open-source repository:
Scenario A (Baseline Open Gateway): scenario-a-baseline-open-gateway.yaml
Scenario B (Microsegmented Hub-and-Spoke): scenario-b-microseg-hub-spoke.yaml
Scenario C (Flat Bidirectional Mesh): scenario-c-mesh-bidirectional.yaml

5. Evaluation & Empirical Findings
5.1 Control-Plane & Throughput Scaling (50 to 500 QPS)
We first evaluate system scaling under Scenario A (Open Gateway), varying container creation rates from 50 to 500 QPS across the 720-node cluster up to 35,000 pods.
Container Provisioning Latency (Schedule-to-Run)
