Analysis of Provisioning Scaling
Cilium Provisioning Agility in Scenario A: Under the open gateway topology, Cilium maintains low and consistent provisioning latency (1.49s P50 and 5.48s P99 at 500 QPS). Because no dynamic peer selector expands peer identities into local endpoint BPF maps, each container requires strictly 3 static egress entries, allowing local BPF map allocations to proceed without lock contention.
Kindnet Scaling & Runtime Co-Design: Kindnet achieves near-parity at 500 QPS (2.35s P50 and 9.72s P99). Reaching this sub-10s P99 latency required resolving the informer cache synchronization bottleneck (illustrated in Figure 1 below):
Baseline CNI Bottleneck: Without runtime interception, standard CNI invocation blocked waiting for asynchronous informer cache sync, ballooning P50 latency to 57.70s (and P99 to 83.60s).
NRI Lifecycle Interception & Synchronous Runtime Hooks: Integrating a synchronous containerd NRI socket plugin decoupled container execution from CNI polling (dropping P50 from 57.70s to 9.77s), while direct in-kernel set reconciliation brought P50 down to 2.35s and P99 under 10 seconds (9.72s, an 8.6x P99 latency reduction).

