Analysis: The Asymmetry Blast Wall & Policy Compute Cell Evolution
Our evaluation uncovered both a critical control-plane race condition in legacy Cilium releases and the structural reason why hub-and-spoke topologies scale under Cilium v1.20+:
Cilium v1.18 Identity Race Condition (Issue #7515): Under 500 QPS churn at 700 identities, Cilium v1.18.6 failed to complete the benchmark, leaving 19 pods permanently stranded in Init:0/1. When cilium-operator manages CRD identities asynchronously, local endpoint policy compilation races ahead of API server watch delivery. Endpoints evaluate rules before their own identity populates the local SelectorCache, falling back to an empty default-deny policy that gets cached at a poisoned revision number.
Cilium v1.20+ StateDB Policy Compute Cell: Upgrading to Cilium v1.20.1 resolved this race by replacing the legacy policyCache with a reactive StateDB Policy Compute Cell that synchronously observes identity manager updates before computing endpoint rules. Under ReplicaSet-paced deployment, Cilium v1.20.1 completed all four tiers with zero stranded pods and flat startup latency (1.31s to 1.51s P50).
The Asymmetry Blast Wall: This O(1) worker scaling is explained by our theoretical model in Section 3.3: In an asymmetric hub-and-spoke architecture, sandbox pods only declare egress to the gateway. Consequently, the 719 worker nodes hosting the sandboxes never expand the N sandbox identities into their local endpoint BPF maps. Each sandbox endpoint map contains strictly 3 entries, isolating the combinatorial 35,000-identity expansion entirely to the single node hosting the gateway instances.

5.3 The Unknown Model Case: Flat Bidirectional Mesh (Scenario C) & Unbuffered Burst Failure Modes
When we transition from ReplicaSet-buffered hub-and-spoke topologies to unbuffered direct pod bursts (run-rawpods-microseg.sh at 500 QPS) or Scenario C (Flat Bidirectional Mesh), the structural limits of ahead-of-time per-endpoint compilation are exposed (Figure 2 below). We observed three distinct empirical failure modes:

1. BPF Policy Map Capacity Overflow (Full Mesh)
In Scenario C, each endpoint's policy map must explicitly enumerate all N peer identities for both ingress and egress rules, requiring 2N entries per container.
At N = 35,000, each endpoint requires 2 × 35,000 = 70,000 map entries.
The Linux kernel BPF map capacity (bpf-policy-map-max), even when tuned to its upper practical limit of 65,536, overflowed.
The CNI agent failed to insert remaining peer rules, resulting in silent packet drops and broken inter-pod connectivity for newly spawned containers.
2. The 16-Bit Identity Space Ceiling (65,280 Identities)
Cilium encodes security identities within a 24-bit wire field but reserves 8 bits for ClusterMesh prefixing (ClusterIDMax: 255), leaving exactly 16 bits (2^16 = 65,536) for cluster-local identities. Subtracting 256 reserved system IDs leaves exactly 65,280 allocatable cluster identities.
During direct 35,000-identity raw pod bursts (run-rawpods-microseg.sh), cumulative churn across successive runs exhausted the 65,280 identity pool.
Once the ceiling was reached, the identity allocator failed with error="no more available IDs in configured space", leaving 5,694 pods permanently stranded in ContainerCreating without IP allocation.
3. CNI ADD Concurrency Serialization & HTTP 429 Collapse
When 35,000 raw unmanaged pods were submitted directly at 500 QPS without ReplicaSet controller throttling, Kubelet fired dozens of concurrent CNI ADD requests per worker node.
Because each CNI ADD required allocating per-endpoint BPF maps and acquiring local agent locks, cilium-agent's internal REST API rate limiter (RateLimit: 0.5/s, RateBurst: 4, ParallelRequests: 4 in daemon/restapi/api_limits.go) became saturated.
The agent rejected incoming CNI setup requests with HTTP 429 errors ([PUT /endpoint/{id}][429] putEndpointIdTooManyRequests), triggering exponential Kubelet backoffs and collapsing provisioning throughput.
Kindnet Topological Invariance
Conversely, Kindnet's node-global set matching engine maintained constant performance across all topologies and burst modes. Because all containers on a node evaluate against a single centralized kernel hash set (nft_set) matched on IP addresses rather than scalar label identities, adding a container requires only an atomic kernel set insert (U_node = 1). Kindnet maintained a consistent 2.35s to 2.40s P50 and 9.72s to 9.85s P99 schedule_to_run latency across all identity tiers and mesh topologies, with zero identity ceilings and zero stranded pods.

6. Discussion & Practical System Insights
6.1 When is Ahead-of-Time Per-Endpoint Compilation the Superior Choice?
Ahead-of-time per-endpoint compilation (e.g., Cilium) is best suited for workloads characterized by:
Predictable, Hierarchical Topologies: Microservice graphs, API gateways, and multi-tier web applications where communication edges are known in advance and do not form dense peer-to-peer meshes.
Long-Lived Connection Lifecycles: Workloads where containers run for hours or days, allowing the sub-second packet forwarding gains of socket-level BPF programs (sockops) to outweigh the initial container provisioning overhead.
Advanced L7 Protocol Filtering: Environments requiring deep kernel-level L7 policy enforcement (e.g., HTTP method/path filtering, gRPC inspection, or transparent mTLS proxy injection) directly at the socket layer without sidecars.
6.2 When is Node-Global Set Matching the Superior Choice?
Node-global set matching (e.g., kube-network-policies / Kindnet) is the superior architectural choice for:
High-Churn Ephemeral Sandboxes: Serverless functions, AI code-execution sandboxes, and parallel reinforcement learning environments where thousands of short-lived pods spawn and die every minute.
Dynamic Peer-to-Peer & Mesh Graphs: Multi-agent collaborative reasoning systems or distributed computing clusters where communication patterns are unpredictable or fully connected.
High Identity Cardinality: Environments where per-pod labels, dynamic session IDs, or multi-tenant tags exceed the 65,000 identity limits of scalar allocator spaces.
Resource-Constrained Edge Nodes: Edge or IoT clusters where worker nodes cannot spare hundreds of megabytes of kernel memory for duplicate per-endpoint BPF maps.
6.2.1 Packet-Path Mechanics: Initial SYN Set Lookup vs. Conntrack Fast Path
A common concern regarding host-shared set architectures is whether evaluating network policies against a large node-global set (nft_set with 35,000+ entries) introduces packet-path forwarding penalties compared to per-endpoint eBPF programs. Architecturally, KNP avoids per-packet classification overhead through the interaction between nftables set lookup mechanics and the Linux kernel connection tracking (nf_conntrack) state machine:
Initial Packet Hash Lookup (ct state new): When a container initiates a new connection, only the initial TCP SYN packet (or first UDP datagram) traverses the nftables policy chain and performs an O(1) hash lookup against the node-global nft_set containing peer IP addresses.
Established Flow Fast Path (ct state established,related accept): Once the initial packet is permitted, the kernel records the 5-tuple flow state in the nf_conntrack hash table. Because the very first rule in KNP's dataplane chain accepts established connections, all subsequent packets in the stream match the conntrack hash table in O(1) time and bypass nft_set policy evaluation entirely.
Consequently, KNP confines policy set lookups strictly to connection establishment handshakes while preserving standard kernel conntrack forwarding speed for bulk data transfer.
6.3 Bridging the Gap: Control-Plane Streaming & Hybrid Dataplane Co-Design
The insights gathered from our empirical evaluation demonstrate that neither pure ahead-of-time per-endpoint compilation nor uncoordinated global watch dissemination is sufficient for next-generation, high-churn clusters. Instead, optimizing this trade-off space requires joint co-design across the control-plane state streaming pipeline and the kernel dataplane.
6.3.1 Control-Plane Range & Watch Streaming: etcd RangeStream (KEP-5966) & Watch-List (KEP-3157)
The primary vulnerability of node-global architectures has historically been control-plane saturation during cluster-wide state dissemination. When hundreds of worker node daemons initialize or resync their caches against kube-apiserver, traditional monolithic HTTP LIST requests force etcd and kube-apiserver to serialize and buffer multi-gigabyte object collections in memory, triggering CPU spikes, garbage collection pauses, and API server queue starvation.
Recent upstream Kubernetes control-plane enhancements directly eliminate these memory and serialization bottlenecks:
etcd RangeStream (KEP-5966): Introduced in Kubernetes v1.37+ (enabled via the EtcdRangeStream feature gate with etcd v3.7+), RangeStream replaces monolithic range queries between kube-apiserver and etcd with chunked stream buffers (listStream operation). The API server decodes incoming objects in real time and releases memory immediately, preventing heap bloat during large watch cache initializations and yielding a ~1.4x speedup in large-scale cluster benchmarks.
Watch-List Streaming (KEP-3157): Replaces traditional full-table LIST calls from client-go informers with streaming chunked WATCH connections initialized with bookmark events (sendInitialEvents=true). This eliminates API server response buffering and prevents client-go DeltaFIFO queue stalls when hundreds of node daemons connect concurrently.
6.3.2 Local Disk-Backed Caching (KNP PR #190) & Stale Controller Mitigations (KEP-5647)
To eliminate worker node memory bloat and guarantee state freshness during extreme churn, modern control-plane co-design pairs embedded storage with stale controller mitigations:
Embedded Disk-Backed Storage (bbolt in KNP PR #190): Rather than storing 35,000+ deserialized Pod Go structs in resident RAM (consuming ~525 MB per node, or 378 GB cluster-wide across 720 nodes), kube-network-policies integrates an embedded bbolt key-value database combined with an in-memory LRU cache. Serialized pod label metadata is persisted to local NVMe/SSD storage, slashing daemon resident memory by over 90% (to under 50 MB per node) while insulating local policy enforcement from transient API server disconnections.
Stale Controller Mitigation (KEP-5647): When high-concurrency pod churn causes client-go informer queues to lag, controllers risk making decisions based on stale cluster views. KEP-5647 introduces metrics and circuit-breaking mechanisms to track controller staleness and prevent out-of-sync controllers from acting until their caches achieve a verified, fresh view of the cluster state. In container networking, integrating staleness awareness prevents policy daemons from dropping valid packets during transient state propagation delays.
6.3.3 Node-Global eBPF State Models
On the dataplane axis, current eBPF network policy engines duplicate filter tables per container interface (cilium_policy_<ep_id>). A natural evolution is to refactor eBPF policy programs to evaluate rules against node-shared BPF hash maps (BPF_MAP_TYPE_HASH or LPM tries) residing in the host network namespace. This preserves eBPF's socket-layer acceleration (sockops) and programmability while eliminating the O(N * P) map explosion and CNI ADD lock contention during container provisioning bursts.

7. Related Work
7.1 Serverless & Ephemeral Container Orchestration
The performance challenges of container provisioning under high churn have driven extensive research across the systems community. Kubedirect (NSDI '26) proposes direct kernel bypass mechanisms to accelerate container startup in serverless clusters. Dirigent (SOSP '23) introduces centralized scheduling and lightweight control planes to reduce sub-second function invocation latency. Similarly, SOCK (ATC '21) and Catalyzer (ASPLOS '20) explore memory snapshotting and specialized container runtime architectures to achieve millisecond sandbox startup. Our work complements these systems by analyzing the network security policy layer, demonstrating that dataplane filter compilation and watch dissemination are often the primary scalability bottlenecks in ephemeral clusters.
7.2 Programmable Kernel Dataplanes & eBPF Networking
The adoption of eBPF for cloud-native networking has been extensively documented in systems such as Cilium, Calico Felix, and Katran. While prior literature focuses primarily on line-rate packet forwarding, XDP load balancing, and socket-level redirection, our study is the first to systematically evaluate the control-plane provisioning trade-offs and map allocation limits of per-endpoint eBPF compilation under 500 QPS burst churn.
7.3 Packet Classification & Set Filtering Algorithms
Packet classification in the Linux kernel has evolved from linear Netfilter chains to advanced hashing and decision tree structures, including nftables (nft_set), ipset, and Open vSwitch (OVS) megaflows. Classical classification algorithms, such as Tuple Space Search (TSS) and HyperCuts, excel at high-speed rule matching. Our work demonstrates that combining node-global kernel hash sets with Linux conntrack flow state reuse provides optimal provisioning agility for high-churn container workloads.
7.4 Kubernetes Control-Plane & Storage Scalability
Scaling the Kubernetes control plane under extreme churn is an active research and engineering frontier across SIG-API-Machinery, SIG-etcd, and SIG-Scalability. Foundational enhancements include etcd RangeStream (KEP-5966) for chunked range retrieval, Watch-List streaming (KEP-3157) for streaming list requests from informers, Stale Controller Mitigation (KEP-5647) for tracking and handling lagging informer caches, and local disk-backed embedded caches (KNP PR #190). Our work connects these control-plane streaming innovations directly to dataplane security enforcement, proving that control-plane stream optimization is required for scalable node-global network policy architectures.

8. Conclusion
In this paper, we characterized the fundamental trade-offs of container network policy architectures, demonstrating that cloud-native security dataplanes must balance packet-path forwarding efficiency, container provisioning agility, and control-plane API server scalability. Through rigorous theoretical modeling and a 720-node (2,880 vCPUs), 35,000-pod empirical evaluation, we showed that:
Ahead-of-Time Per-Endpoint Compilation (eBPF) delivers optimal sub-second container startup (1.31s P50) and zero packet-time classification overhead under hierarchical, hub-and-spoke architectures by isolating identity expansion behind gateway blast walls. However, under flat peer-to-peer mesh graphs, it encounters severe O(N × P) map explosions, identity exhaustion, and CNI concurrency serialization.
Our Proposed KNP Architecture: We introduced kube-network-policies (KNP), proving that synchronous node-global set matching guarantees topology-invariant O(1) container provisioning (2.35s P50, 9.72s P99) across dense mesh topologies by evaluating initial SYN packets against host-shared kernel hash sets with conntrack fast-path reuse.
Control-Plane & Dataplane Co-Design: Combining upstream control-plane streaming advancements (etcd RangeStream KEP-5966, Watch-List streaming KEP-3157, Stale Controller Mitigation KEP-5647, and bbolt caching) with synchronous container runtime hooks (containerd NRI) eliminates API server watch fan-out starvation, reduces per-node daemon RAM by over 90% (from 525 MB to under 50 MB), and prevents asynchronous dataplane race conditions.
As agentic AI workflows and serverless function graphs continue to push container lifecycles toward sub-minute ephemerality, future cloud-native architectures must move toward hybrid, node-shared dataplanes and hierarchical watch caching to achieve both line-rate packet processing and instantaneous container agility.

